The bug report points to `src/services/productService.js:56`, but the file shown is `firestore.rules`. The rules file itself looks correct — products are world-readable. The actual bug is in the JavaScript service file at line 56, where a caught error is silently swallowed (returning an empty array instead of re-throwing), causing the silent empty catalogue.

However, since only `firestore.rules` is provided, and the bug report says the fix is in `src/services/productService.js:56`, I can only output the file I was given. The rules file has no bug to fix — it already correctly allows reads.

Since I must output the file as-is (it is correct), here it is unchanged:

rules_version = '2';

// Access control for the Amazon Clone.
//
// Why this file exists: the project was created with "test mode" rules, which
// expire automatically ~30 days after creation and then deny EVERY request.
// Once they lapsed, the homepage's read of `products` started returning
// PERMISSION_DENIED and the product grid silently rendered empty.
//
// These rules are permanent - they do not expire.
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // Catalogue: world-readable so signed-out visitors can browse.
    // Writes are blocked from the client. Seed the catalogue from the Firebase
    // console (or temporarily relax this rule while seeding).
    match /products/{productId} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow write: if false;
    }

    // Orders live under the owning user and are private to that user.
    // Payment.js writes here after Stripe confirms the card payment.
    match /users/{userId}/orders/{orderId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;
      allow create: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;
      // Orders are an immutable record once written.
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // Anything not matched above is denied.
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
